  Next: 05-cost.bl — what it costs, and why that decides the backend

── store ranges per read, at two sizes ──
  operation                   N=10      N=40      shape
  entity (point read)         1         1         CONSTANT
  column scan                 1         1         CONSTANT
  two-clause join             2         2         CONSTANT
  three-clause join           3         3         CONSTANT
  pull (2 attributes)         3         3         CONSTANT
  aggregate (count)           1         1         CONSTANT
  as-of column scan           1         1         CONSTANT

── what that table says ──
  Every read is CONSTANT in store operations.

  The join was not always. It used to evaluate each clause once
  PER BINDING ROW: 11 ranges at N=10, 41 at N=40. Against ETS a
  range is a local call and nobody notices. Against a networked
  store a join over 1000 entities is 1001 round trips — at
  0.5ms RTT, half a second of latency for data that fits in one
  packet.

  Each pattern is now scanned ONCE for the whole binding set and
  unified in memory. That is the single most important number
  here for choosing a backend.

── store operations per bulk transaction ──
  entities      ranges      updates     commits
  10            62          11          1
  20            122         21          1
  40            242         41          1

── what that table says ──
  Writes are LINEAR, not constant — a transaction must check
  what each entity currently holds. Doubling the transaction
  doubles the work, which is the property that makes bulk
  loading possible at all.

  One COMMIT per transaction, whatever its size. Every datom
  reaches the store together or not at all; a transaction that
  committed per operation could be observed half-applied.

── wall-clock, for orientation only ──
  operation                   ms
  (over 200 entities)
  column scan                 27
  two-clause join             58
  aggregate                   17
  point read                  0
  bulk transact               614

── how to read those numbers ──
  Do not. Not as thresholds, anyway.

  They are here to catch a change that makes something
  catastrophically slower, not to establish what is fast. On a
  loaded machine the same run varies by more than 2x, so any
  conclusion drawn from a single number is a conclusion about
  the machine.

  The COUNTS above are the contract. test/bl/datom/cost_test.bl
  asserts them, so a regression appears as a failing number
  rather than as a benchmark somebody has to interpret.

── what this means for the backend ──
  Reads are O(1) in store operations, so a networked store
  costs one round trip per read regardless of database size.

  Writes are O(n) in the size of the TRANSACTION. A large
  import is many round trips today; batching the per-operation
  lookups into one range over the affected span is the obvious
  next optimisation, and it is filed as FEAT-011.

  The store protocol is six methods. Everything above it is
  pure logic over an ordered key/value space, so what the
  substrate must provide is: ordered keys, prefix range scans,
  atomic multi-key commit, and compare-and-swap. Anything
  offering those can carry this database unchanged.

── the store is a parameter ──
  database file:  /tmp/example-datom-19298.fjall
  native backend loaded:  true

── storing terms Datomic has no type for ──
  ✓ a tuple — round-tripped: {:ok, 200, "created"}
  ✓ a nested map — round-tripped: {:flags #{:tls :http2}, :server {:port 8080, :host "localhost"}, :retries [100 200 400]}
  ✓ a deep vector — round-tripped: [1 [2 [3 [4 [5]]]]]
  ✓ a set — round-tripped: #{:read :write :admin}
  ✓ a bignum — round-tripped: 123456789012345678901234567890
  ✓ a float — round-tripped: 3.14159265358979
  ✓ an erlang list — round-tripped: (:a :b :c)

── found by value ──
  by tuple:  #{["a tuple"]}
  by set (built backwards):  #{["a set"]}
  by nested map:  #{["a nested map"]}

── an index is an ORDER, not a hash ──
  {9}                   <  {1, 1}    (language says -1)
  [1]                   <  [1 2]    (language says -1)
  {:a 1}                <  {:a 2}    (language says -1)
  1                     <  3.14    (language says -1)
  42                    <  123456789012345678901234567890    (language says -1)

  Every one of those matches the language's own `compare`,
  which is what makes a range scan over such a column mean
  anything at all. It is checked exhaustively over 1,225 pairs
  in test/bl/datom/terms_test.bl.

── it is still there when the process is gone ──
  written, and the file is on disk:  /tmp/example-datom-19298.fjall

  A separate OS process, a fresh BEAM VM, opening the same path
  reads this back — the data, the schema, the transaction basis,
  and the full history. `connect-with` recovers all four from the
  store rather than assuming an empty one, which is what makes a
  restart a restart rather than a silent fresh start.

  See test/bl/datom/conformance_test.bl, whose restart tests run
  against every store implementation — including this one.

── the whole stack ──
  priv/lib/datom/*.bl            17 files   the database
  native/datom_fjall/src     1 crate    transactions, durability
  Elixir modules for datom   0

  `defnative` lets a beam-lisp namespace host a NIF directly, so
  the storage backend needs no Elixir wrapper. Elixir keeps only
  what is genuinely substrate — and a NIF is substrate, while the
  DECLARATION that one exists belongs beside the code using it.

── what to take away ──
  The value type is any BEAM term, not a closed list
  Composites are INDEXED, not just serialized
  Equal values encode identically; different ones never collide
  The store is a parameter: ETS, a sorted map, or fjall on disk
  Restart recovers data, schema, basis and history

── 1. Ten processes, ten questions, one instant in time ────────────────
    :count-all → #{[60]}
    :pending → #{[40]}
    :shipped → #{[20]}
    :revenue → #{[17700]}
    :largest → #{[590]}

  Five processes. One instant. No locks, no isolation level, no
  coordination — because there was nothing to coordinate.

── 2. Twenty concurrent writers, and a reader that does not flinch ─────
  the value taken before the writes: #{[60]}
  the value taken after them:        #{[80]}

  Both are correct. They are answers to different questions,
  and each says WHICH question it answered — which is what a
  timestamp on a report is supposed to mean and rarely does.

── 3. A db value as process state ──────────────────────────────────────
  a worker computed its report from a world of its own:
    basis 1000001 — total #{[17700]}
  meanwhile the connection has moved on to basis 1000021

── 4. A process registry that remembers ────────────────────────────────
  who is the coordinator?
    #{["shard-0"]}
  what is THIS pid doing?   #PID<0.51850.0>
    #{["shard-0"]}
  the pid as a lookup ref → {:db/id 4, :w/role :coordinator, :w/task "shard-0"}

── 5. What was it doing before? ────────────────────────────────────────
  now:     shard-9 (rebalanced)
  before:  shard-0

  every task this process has held:
     ＋ shard-0  @ tx 1000001
     － shard-0  @ tx 1000002
     ＋ shard-9 (rebalanced)  @ tx 1000002

── 6. A process dies, and the database remembers what it was doing ─────
  alive?  false
  is it still a worker?     nil
  what was it doing though: shard-doomed
  and the pid is still a value we can hold: #PID<0.51993.0>

  A dead process leaves no trace on the BEAM — that is the point
  of isolation. Here it leaves exactly the trace we chose to
  record, and no more.

── the shape of it ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Datomic: a database is a value, so readers need no locks.
  The BEAM: a process is cheap, so give everything its own.

  Neither idea needs the other to be true. But a value that
  costs nothing to hold, in a runtime that makes a process for
  every request, means every request can have its own
  consistent world for free — and that is not a feature either
  system lists, because neither can see it alone.


── 1. A worker crashes mid-job; its world does not ─────────────────────
  the worker: false
  the job it claimed: :running

  A supervisor restarting that worker can see EXACTLY what it
  had done: `:running` with no completion is a claim nobody
  fulfilled. That is a recoverable state rather than a mystery,
  and it did not need a write-ahead log to be one.

── 2. What should the supervisor retry? ────────────────────────────────
  jobs claimed but never finished:
    job 2 — attempts so far: 0

  In a mutable store this is the question you cannot trust,
  because `:running` might mean `running right now` or `was
  running when something died`. Here you can ask WHEN it became
  running, and compare that to whether the process still lives.

── 3. One handle_call instead of twenty ────────────────────────────────
  a process holding a db value can answer questions its author
  never enumerated:
    how many jobs?  #{[5]}
    which states?   #{[:queued] [:running]}
    one job, shaped: {:db/id 6, :job/id 2, :job/state :running}
    a wrongly-SHAPED request is declined by the clause head:
                     [:not-a-query :not-even-a-query]

  Three questions, three handle-call clauses, and the server was
  written before any of the questions existed. It holds a
  database, not a schema of supported calls.

  The guard declines on SHAPE, which is the most a guard can do
  and exactly what belongs in a clause head. Whether a
  well-shaped query MEANS anything is the body's problem, and
  OTP already has an answer for that one.

── 4. A migration you can check afterwards ─────────────────────────────
  jobs with a priority, after:  #{[4]}
  jobs with a priority, before: #{}

  The old state is still there to compare against — not in a
  backup, not in a migration log, just still there. A
  `code_change/3` that got it wrong is a diff, not an incident.

── 5. A thousand requests, a thousand consistent worlds ────────────────
  200 processes each took their own database value
  distinct answers among them: 1
  → #{#{[5]}}

  One answer, because nothing was writing. Had something been,
  there would be several — and each would still be CORRECT, as
  of the moment its process asked. That is the difference
  between a stale read and a consistent one.

── the shape of it ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  OTP's questions are all about failure: what survives a crash,
  what a supervisor restores, what a hot upgrade preserves.

  Every one of those answers gets simpler when the thing being
  recovered is an immutable value with a name. Not because the
  database is clever, but because there is nothing to repair —
  a crashed process cannot have left a value half-written.

  `let it crash` and `never destroy a fact` are the same
  instinct, arrived at from opposite directions.


