  breaking:  [{:value "d@x", :check :unique, :attr :person/email, :entities [4 5]}]
  recorded?  false
  · the migration author's data assumption was wrong — refused, not
  · corrupted, with the offending value named

── what to take away ──
  A migration is one value: schema deltas, invariants, remediation
  plan walks a db VALUE — including production — and commits nothing
  apply! remediates, re-checks, grows and records as ONE transaction
  the ledger is a query; applying twice is a no-op
  an under-remediating migration is refused, not applied halfway

  This rests on two primitives the earlier examples lacked:
  `with` (speculative transaction) and `:db/fn` (transaction
  functions) — see 10-with-efficiency.bl and 11-txfn-efficiency.bl
  for how each works and what it costs.

── correctness: `with` does not touch the base ──
  people in base before:  5
  people in speculative db-after:  6
  people in base AFTER the with:  5
  ghost visible only in speculation:  true
  · the overlay saw base+ghost; the base never learned the ghost existed
  clone strategy agrees:  true

── cost of one migration dry-run (hits against BASE) ──
  strategy      N=20 trips    N=20 ent      N=80 trips    N=80 ent      
  OVERLAY       10            40            10            160           
  CLONE         1             141           1             501           

── reading the table ──
  · OVERLAY: base round trips = number of dry-run SCANS (touches only
  ·          the columns the migration reads); entries grow with those
  ·          columns. Zero cost for columns the migration never checks.
  · CLONE:   one big round trip up front returning the ENTIRE keyspace
  ·          (~all datoms); then every scan is local (0 further base).
  · 
  · ∴ OVERLAY wins for a targeted dry-run against a large prod store:
  ·   it transfers only the columns under test. CLONE wins only when a
  ·   session dry-runs MANY migrations touching MOST columns — then the
  ·   one-time copy amortizes. For CI 'plan this PR's migration', overlay.
  · 
  · The design's `with` should therefore default to OVERLAY, exposing
  · CLONE for the batch-audit case. Both are ~40 lines; neither edits a
  · layer above the store port.

── why not just transact into base and undo? ──
  · base is ETS: a shared mutable table with no transactions (store-
  · ets.bl says so). A 'write then retract' speculative would be VISIBLE
  · to concurrent readers mid-flight and unrecoverable on crash. The
  · overlay keeps speculation in a per-call buffer the base never sees —
  · the same reason a db is a VALUE: speculation must not be a place.

── takeaway ──
  `with` = the writer pipeline minus the basis advance ✓ (runs above)
  non-mutating against a shared base ✓ (overlay buffer)
  two strategies, measured: OVERLAY for targeted dry-run,
  CLONE for batch audit — pick per workload, both tiny
  unblocks full-migration dry-run (fn + grow) against prod

── a tx-fn reads current state, writes the delta ──
  balance before:  100
  balance after +50:  150
  commits for the deposit:  1
  · the fn READ 100 and WROTE 150 — a read-modify-write, one commit

── migration remediation: 3 commits vs 1 ──
  entities holding dup@x:  2
  OLD way commits (remediate + stamp):  2
  NEW way commits (fn + stamp, folded):  1
  dup@x now held by:  1
  · one commit = one crash-atomic unit. Folding the fn's retractions
  · and the ledger stamp into a single transact! halves the windows a
  · crash can land in — and with schema datoms folded too (below), the
  · WHOLE migration becomes one commit.

── the last mile: schema grow in the SAME commit ──
  · install-schema! is its own writer turn (conn.bl), so today a
  · migration is: [fn+stamp commit] then [schema-grow commit]. To make
  · the ENTIRE migration one commit, the schema deltas must ride as
  · datoms in the same transact! — which schema.bl already supports:
  · `definition-datoms` turns an attr def into [:db/add e a v] ops.
  · So a fully-atomic migration is:
  · 
  ·   (transact! conn
  ·     (concat (expand-txfns db [[:db/fn 'dedup-email]])   ; remediate
  ·             (definition-datoms e new-email-def)          ; grow
  ·             [ledger-stamp]))                             ; record
  · 
  · one expand, one validate, one realize, one commit — atomic. The
  · only piece priv/ lacks is teaching tx.bl/expand the :db/fn form and
  · the schema MAP-update side-effect that must accompany schema datoms
  · (so reads see the new cardinality). Both are additive to expand.

── expander cost: linear, one db read per fn ──
  · expand-txfns is O(forms): each :db/fn calls its fn once with the
  · SAME db value (a snapshot, O(1) to hold). A tx-fn's OWN reads are
  · the only store cost, and they are the reads the migration would do
  · anyway. The expander adds no scans — it is a splice, not a query.
  · ∴ tx-fns cost exactly what their body reads; the mechanism is free.

── takeaway ──
  :db/fn = a fn of the db value, spliced into tx-data at expand ✓
  reads current state safely (one snapshot), commits atomically ✓
  folds remediate + stamp (+ schema datoms) into ONE commit —
  closes the Q4 seam 09-migrations.bl left open
  integration point: tx.bl/expand only; everything downstream
  (validate, realize, write-datoms) is unchanged

── 1. reachability (transitive closure) ──
  reachable from A (cycle + tail, terminates):  (["A"] ["B"] ["C"] ["D"] ["E"])
  reachable from A whose name > "B":  (["C"] ["D"] ["E"])

── 2. connected components (recursion + min) ──
  component label per node (min reachable id):  (["A" 3] ["B" 3] ["C" 3] ["P" 6] ["Q" 6])

── 3. shortest path (weighted, recursion + arithmetic + min) ──
  shortest cost from node 1 (note 1→3 is 3, not the direct 10):  ([2 1] [3 3] [4 4])

── 4. same-generation (non-linear recursion) ──
  same generation as c (siblings d, AND cousin e):  (["c"] ["d"] ["e"])

── 5. native fixpoint vs bl fallback (the one-truth speedup) ──
  chain length | bl-fallback ms | native ms | speedup | closure tuples | identical
       10      |      49      |    11    |   4x   |   45   |   true
       20      |      265      |    36    |   7x   |   190   |   true
       30      |      876      |    87    |   10x   |   435   |   true

  → `materialise` dispatches to the NATIVE kernel: bl owns pattern
    meaning + value interning + decode, native owns the whole fixpoint.
    Output is bit-identical to the bl fallback; the speedup grows with
    depth (the boundary is crossed once per query, not per round).

── what to take away ──
  A rule that invokes itself is recursion — the primitive that makes
  this a Datalog: reachability, components, shortest paths, same-gen
  Rules travel through :in as % and invoke like any clause; they JOIN
  Bottom-up fixpoint TERMINATES on cycles where top-down would loop
  min/max in an outer stratum expresses components and shortest paths
  `materialise` dispatches to ONE native fixpoint (bl owns meaning+decode)

