    :order/status → :pending  (retracted)
    :order/status → :shipped  (asserted)

── the shape of it ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  A commit is a message. The log is the topic. The delta is the
  payload — the facts themselves, so the reader never re-queries.

── A server that tracks the database ───────────────────────────────────
  at start:           #{}
  after three writes: #{[2]}
  the server never re-subscribed and never polled — its state
  simply follows the log, one commit at a time.

── the shape of it ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  A db value as GenServer state was already the pattern. `listen!`
  makes it LIVE: `handle-info` adopts the latest world, for free.

── Subscribed to :order/status — deaf to everything else ───────────────
  wrote :order/total only  → woke? false   (want false)
  wrote :order/status      → woke? true    (want true)
    and the delta carried :order/status = :shipped

── No filter means every commit ────────────────────────────────────────
  unfiltered, wrote :order/total → woke? true   (want true)

── the shape of it ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  The cheapest subscription is a set membership test on what
  changed. It reads the delta's attributes, nothing more.

── A subscriber falls behind, then catches up from the log ─────────────
  last confirmed basis: 1000001  (entity 1 already seen)
  newest basis the subscriber actually observed: 1000004
  but it processed them out of order / dropped some — so it
  does NOT trust its incremental state. It replays instead.

── Replay: (since db t0) reconstructs everything after the watermark ───
  entities recovered after t0: (2 3 4)
  entity 1 (before t0) excluded? true   (want true — no double-count)

── the shape of it ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  The broadcast is best-effort; the log is durable and ordered.
  A gap is detectable (the basis) and recoverable (`since`), so
  delivery is effectively exactly-once — stronger than raw send.

── A basis subscriber gets a coordinate, then re-derives ───────────────
  the world moved to basis 1000001
  attributes that changed: #{:order/id :order/status}
  (no datoms in the payload — this subscriber re-derives)
  re-derived: shipped count at basis 1000001 = #{[1]}

── changed-attrs is clean ──────────────────────────────────────────────
  changed-attrs: #{:order/id :order/status}   (no :db/txInstant)

── the shape of it ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Delta by default; basis by knob. A coordinate is right when the
  reader will re-derive anyway — the LiveView case FEAT-016 was
  written for. The writer still emits a fact, never a projection.

