  tab A mounted: <ul><li data-key="a">First</li></ul>
  tab B mounted: <ul><li data-key="a">First</li></ul>

── one write commits — watch BOTH tabs re-render ──
  tab A patch: [[:insert [] "b" 1 [:li {:key "b"} "Second"]]]
  tab B patch: [[:insert [] "b" 1 [:li {:key "b"} "Second"]]]

  the shape of it: neither tab was told. The commit landed on the
  log; each socket re-projected from the new basis. Convergence is
  a property of the log, not a message between the tabs.
── a per-socket cart, backed by a session datom conn ──
  mount: <ul><li data-key="book" data-in-cart="no">The Book</li><li data-key="pen" data-in-cart="no">A Pen</li></ul>
  after add-to-cart: [[:set-attr [0] "data-in-cart" "yes"]]

  the shared catalogue never changed — only this viewer's session.
  the cart is a DATABASE: it has q, pull, as-of, watch for free, and
  it dies with the socket process. Ephemeral state, zero teardown.
── one guarded view, two principals — disjoint rows ──
  alice sees: <ul><li data-key="a1">Alice&#39;s plan</li></ul>
  bob sees:   <ul><li data-key="b1">Bob&#39;s memo</li></ul>
  alice never sees Bob's memo — the store never selected it for her.

── a denied intent never reaches the log ──
  denial pushed: [:denied :read-only]
  docs before: 2  after: 2  (unchanged — no write)

  the shape of it: the view is scoped and the write is gated, both in
  the SAME datalog. The guard's monotonicity is also the live diff's
  soundness gate — one idea securing and accelerating at once.
── optimistic UI: render now, reconcile from the log ──

  optimistic patch (instant): [:patch [[:set-attr [] "data-done" "true"] [:set-attr [] "data-pending" "yes"] [:text [0] "done"]]]
  reconciled from commit:      [:patch [[:set-attr [] "data-pending" "no"]]]

── a denied action rolls the guess back — from the last fact ──
  optimistic (guessed done):   [:patch [[:set-attr [] "data-done" "true"] [:set-attr [] "data-pending" "yes"] [:text [0] "done"]]]
  denial:                      [:denied :forbidden]
  rolled back to committed:    [:patch [[:set-attr [] "data-done" "false"] [:set-attr [] "data-pending" "no"] [:text [0] "open"]]]
  shared world done? false (still false — nothing committed)

  the shape of it: the optimistic value is a local; the authoritative
  value is a fact. Reconcile clears the local on the commit echo, and
  a denial rolls back to the last fact — the log is the rollback source.
── undo/redo is a basis pointer over the session conn ──

  now      (b3): hello world
  undo →   (b2): hello
  undo →   (b1): hel
  redo →   (b3): hello world

  the same VIEW renders any of these — undo is just :at ← prev-basis:
    at b2: <div id="editor" data-basis="1000002"><p>hello</p></div>
    at b3: <div id="editor" data-basis="1000003"><p>hello world</p></div>

  the shape of it: no undo stack, no snapshots, no inverse ops. The
  session log is the history; as-of is the time machine; the view is
  one function over a coordinate. Ctrl-Z = (assign :at prev-basis).
── Pulse: two teammates, one shared world ──
  Ada's tab mounted, feed has the seed check-in
  Bo's tab mounted too.

── Bo clicks "Feeling great" ──
  Ada's tab: (:text :insert)
  Bo's tab:  (:text :insert)
    → :text updated the "N today" count; :insert added Bo's row.
      Exactly the two things that changed — nothing else redrawn.

── the log now holds ──
  @bo · great · design system clicks now
  @ada · great · shipped the keyed differ ✨

  The frontend is a pure function of the world. The backend decides
  what becomes true. They meet at the database — and every viewer
  converges on the same facts, live, through the log.
