── it is a real relation: attribute spelling ──
  ~  ["A budget for investing your cash" "Index funds and the interest rate"]
  ~  ["A budget for investing your cash" "Saving money on a mortgage loan"]
  ~  ["A feline guide to the purring cat" "Caring for your new kitten"]
  ~  ["Adopting a rescue puppy" "Training your dog to sit and stay"]
  ~  ["Caring for your new kitten" "A feline guide to the purring cat"]
  ~  ["Index funds and the interest rate" "Saving money on a mortgage loan"]
  ~  ["Index funds and the interest rate" "The bank raised the savings rate"]
  ~  ["Saving money on a mortgage loan" "A budget for investing your cash"]
  ~  ["Saving money on a mortgage loan" "Index funds and the interest rate"]
  ~  ["The bank raised the savings rate" "Index funds and the interest rate"]
  ~  ["The bank raised the savings rate" "Saving money on a mortgage loan"]
  ~  ["Training your dog to sit and stay" "Adopting a rescue puppy"]

── the SAME relation, joined with a stored fact ──
  pet docs similar to something:  (["A feline guide to the purring cat"] ["Adopting a rescue puppy"] ["Caring for your new kitten"] ["Training your dog to sit and stay"])

── what to take away ──
  A computed relation is DECLARED with defrelation + a provider.
  [?a :~similar ?b] is an edge — it joins with stored facts freely.
  The query names the MEANING (:~similar); the provider (k-NN) is a
  configuration detail the query never sees. Next: its access modes.

── :bf — generate: the neighbours of a bound entity ──
  near kitten:  (["A feline guide to the purring cat"])

── :bb — probe: is THIS pair a semantic edge? ──
  kitten ~ feline-guide?  true
  kitten ~ bank-doc?  false

── :fb — reverse: who has THIS entity as a neighbour? ──
  who is near the feline guide:  (["Caring for your new kitten"])

── what to take away ──
  ONE relation, THREE access patterns — generate, probe, reverse —
  selected by how the query binds the columns (the adornment).
  A vector DB does generate only; a relation does all three because
  the engine unifies against it like any edge. Next: swap the provider.

── provider: knn-adjacency (cosine top-k) ──
  ~  ["A budget for investing your cash" "Saving money on a mortgage loan"]
  ~  ["A feline guide to the purring cat" "Caring for your new kitten"]
  ~  ["Adopting a rescue puppy" "Training your dog to sit and stay"]
  ~  ["Caring for your new kitten" "A feline guide to the purring cat"]

── provider: threshold-adjacency (same :doc/topic) ──
  ~  ["A budget for investing your cash" "Index funds and the interest rate"]
  ~  ["A budget for investing your cash" "Saving money on a mortgage loan"]
  ~  ["A budget for investing your cash" "The bank raised the savings rate"]
  ~  ["A feline guide to the purring cat" "Adopting a rescue puppy"]

── provider: fn-adjacency (cites either way) ──
  ~  ["A budget for investing your cash" "Index funds and the interest rate"]
  ~  ["A budget for investing your cash" "Saving money on a mortgage loan"]
  ~  ["A feline guide to the purring cat" "Caring for your new kitten"]
  ~  ["Adopting a rescue puppy" "Training your dog to sit and stay"]

── provider: union-adjacency (knn ∪ same-topic) ──
  ~  ["A budget for investing your cash" "Index funds and the interest rate"]
  ~  ["A budget for investing your cash" "Saving money on a mortgage loan"]
  ~  ["A budget for investing your cash" "The bank raised the savings rate"]
  ~  ["A feline guide to the purring cat" "Adopting a rescue puppy"]

── what to take away ──
  The query names :~related and NEVER changed across four mechanisms.
  You are not married to k-NN — the provider is configuration. Swap it
  for BM25, a learned scorer, or a hybrid, and every query that
  traverses :~related — including recursive ones — is unaffected.

── is this recursion native? ──
  native-compatible?:  true

── one hop vs the whole chain of meaning ──
  directly similar to kitten:  (["A feline guide to the purring cat"])
  reachable through a CHAIN of meaning from kitten:  (["A feline guide to the purring cat"] ["Caring for your new kitten"])

── it still JOINS with structure ──
  finance docs reachable by meaning from an index-funds doc:  (["A budget for investing your cash"] ["Index funds and the interest rate"] ["Saving money on a mortgage loan"] ["The bank raised the savings rate"])

── what to take away ──
  A computed relation in a RECURSIVE rule runs on the native fixpoint:
  the k-NN graph materialises once as a base relation, then the
  recursion is native. Semantic transitive closure — reachability
  through meaning — at native speed. Next: rank those chains by cost.

── the weighted citation edges (cost = semantic distance) ──
  cost  ["A budget for investing your cash" "Index funds and the interest rate" 60]
  cost  ["A budget for investing your cash" "Saving money on a mortgage loan" 39]
  cost  ["A feline guide to the purring cat" "Caring for your new kitten" 244]
  cost  ["Adopting a rescue puppy" "Training your dog to sit and stay" 399]
  cost  ["Saving money on a mortgage loan" "Index funds and the interest rate" 50]
  cost  ["The bank raised the savings rate" "Index funds and the interest rate" 48]
  cost  ["Training your dog to sit and stay" "Caring for your new kitten" 797]

── cheapest (most coherent) chain from a budget article ──
  min cost to reach each ancestor:  (["Index funds and the interest rate" 60] ["Saving money on a mortgage loan" 39])

── what to take away ──
  A WEIGHTED relation + arithmetic-in-recursion + min = the cheapest
  reasoning chain, ranked. The weight here composes a STORED graph
  (:doc/cites) with COMPUTED distance (embeddings) — two relations
  feeding one native fixpoint. Next: the fixpoint AS a labeller.

── seeds: only two documents are labelled up front ──
  seed  ["Caring for your new kitten" :pets]
  seed  ["Index funds and the interest rate" :finance]

── after propagation: every reachable doc gets a topic ──
  label  ["A budget for investing your cash" :finance]
  label  ["A feline guide to the purring cat" :pets]
  label  ["Caring for your new kitten" :pets]
  label  ["Index funds and the interest rate" :finance]
  label  ["Saving money on a mortgage loan" :finance]
  label  ["The bank raised the savings rate" :finance]

── what to take away ──
  Two seed labels + one recursive rule over :~similar = a running
  label-propagation / graph-SSL algorithm. The FIXPOINT is the
  algorithm — no separate ML step. Add a seed, rerun, labels respread.
  Next: multi-hop retrieval that carries its own justification.

