── what a separate vector database would require ──
  A bolt-on vector database knows its current vectors, not datom history.
  Reproducing each old answer means snapshotting and reindexing that store
  for every point in time, then keeping its ids aligned with the fact DB.
  Here the transaction basis is already the history and already the index bound.

── what to take away ──
  Embeddings are datoms, so semantic search inherits database time travel
  as-of changes the visible corpus; the similar-to clause needs no special flag
  Vectors and joined facts are read at one transaction-consistent basis
  Historical vector answers need no copied corpus and no rebuilt external index

