── three surface forms, one machine ──
  raw receive-loop:   ([:dec "(- n 1)"] [:inc "(+ n 1)"] [:reset "0"])
  defserver (OTP):    ([:dec "(- n 1)"] [:inc "(+ n 1)"] [:reset "0"])
  defmulti dispatch:  ([:dec "(- n 1)"] [:inc "(+ n 1)"] [:reset "0"])
  all three identical: true

── the guard is reported where the source writes it — never fabricated ──
  inc → (+ n 1)
  dec → (- n 1)   [when (> n 0)]
  reset → 0

── the naming trichotomy — every machine has a stable identity ──
  defserver, by binding:       counter
  anonymous loop, content-hash: ↻⟨dec|inc|reset⟩·4092

  → verification is written ONCE over the transition graph; every
    dispatch form inherits it. defserver gets the whole battery, unchanged.

── a correct bank account: □(balance ≥ 0) proven unbounded ──
  init establishes balance ≥ 0:   true
  deposit preserves it: true
  withdraw preserves it: true
  □(balance ≥ 0) HOLDS: true — for every balance and every amount

── a bug: withdraw without the balance ≥ amt guard ──
  holds: false — the checker caught the missing guard
  counterexample (state+amount that overdraws):
    (
      (define-fun amt () Int
        1)
      (define-fun balance2 () Int
        (- 1))
      (define-fun balance () Int
        0)
    )
    

  → not 'unsound' — the exact overdrawing state, from the source.

── abduce the missing precondition for an unguarded withdraw ──
  miniKanren enumerated, z3 decided.
  sufficient preconditions (shortest first): ((<= amt balance) (<= amt 0) (>= balance amt))
  → e.g. (<= amt balance) — a non-positive withdrawal cannot overdraw.

── domain-relative honesty: too small a vocabulary → no fabricated proof ──
  candidates over {amt} only: [] — nothing, honestly.
  widen to {balance amt 0}:   ((<= amt balance)) — the fix appears.

── the discharge only we can do: does every SENDER already guarantee it? ──
  all callers guarantee (>= balance amt): true → NO annotation needed.
  after one careless caller: false → the exact site is found.

  → TLA+ cannot do this: it has no callers. We have the whole call graph.

── the rung is recovered — the footprint model regresses nothing ──
  (deref a)  →  footprint {"a" :R}  rung pure
  (swap! a inc)  →  footprint {"a" :W}  rung atom
  (erlang/send p m)  →  footprint {"p" :S}  rung process
  (println x)  →  footprint {:io :W}  rung io

── purity = footprint touches only :R  (refinement: pure ops name no resource) ──
  (+ (deref a) (count xs)) → {"a" :R}  pure? true
  (swap! a inc)            → {"a" :W}  pure? false

── monotonicity = footprint touches only :R/:A  (MVP-F's live-query gate) ──
  (datom/transact! conn tx) → {"conn" :A}  monotone? true
  (swap! cache assoc k v)   → {"cache" :W}  monotone? false

── the frame rule: disjoint resources ⟹ independent, decided structurally ──
  :orders handler ⋈ :balance handler frame-independent? true

  → the :orders handler CANNOT break an invariant over :balance —
    local reasoning, no annotation. This is what makes it SCALE.

── the fork tree IS a fleet non-interference proof ──
  tenant A caps #{orders}   work footprint {"orders" :W}  ⊆ caps? true
  tenant B caps #{inventory} work footprint {"inventory" :W}  ⊆ caps? true
  A-work ⋈ B-work commute? true
  → disjoint caps ⟹ disjoint footprints ⟹ all A-workers commute with
    all B-workers. A thousand-worker fleet, race-free, from the fork tree.

── an un-conveyed spawn escalates to :opaque-world — the confused deputy, caught ──
  (erlang/spawn (fn …))          footprint {:opaque-world :X}  ⊆ #{x}? false
  (erlang/spawn (bind env (fn …))) footprint {}  (bounded)

  → the un-conveyed spawn escapes any finite cap set → flagged; conveying
    it via capture/bind keeps the child within the granted authority.

── an acyclic call graph — provably deadlock-free ──
  gateway→{auth,cache}, auth→store  (a DAG)
  deadlocked servers: []

── a synchronous call cycle — the deadlock, named ──
  orders→billing→shipping→orders (cycle), +orders→audit
  deadlocked servers: ("billing" "orders" "shipping")
  → the three cycle members named; audit (acyclic) excluded.

── break the cycle: make one call async → healed, same query confirms ──
  deadlocked servers: []

  → TLA+ reports a deadlock STATE after search; we report the structural
    CYCLE — the actual cause, from the call graph directly.

── a good handler: no lasso ≤ 20 → live up to the bound (APPROXIMATE) ──
  searched to depth 20 , lasso found? false

── a buggy handler: :retry spins forever → the lasso is found, with a witness ──
  trace: [:idle :pending :pending] , lasso at depth 2
  → a concrete stuck cycle (:pending → :pending, no :replied). Sound.

── honesty is structural: exact vs approximate is a query ──
  approximate guarantees: (:~replies-eventually)
  exact guarantees:       (:~balance-nonneg)

  → no guarantee lies about its own strength.

── optimization: a batched counter refines the simple one ──
  batched tick c+1 simulates abstract tick n+1 under R(n,c)=(n=c): true
  → so balance≥0 and every property proven on the SIMPLE counter transfers.

── protocol conformance: a connection refines the open/close protocol ──
  the connection's phase tracks the protocol on open: true
  → it can NEVER emit a message out of order. The abstract machine IS
    the session type.

── VERIFIED HOT UPGRADE — code_change from v1 {:balance} to v2 {:balance :currency} ──
  1. migration b2 := b1 preserves balance ≥ 0: true
  2. v2.withdraw simulates v1.withdraw (observable balance identical): true
  3. a migration that zeroes the balance is caught: true

  → the hot upgrade is VERIFIED end to end. code_change preserves R, v2
    simulates v1, and balance≥0 — proven on v1 — survives the LIVE swap.
    A wrong migration is rejected BEFORE it ships, the process never
    stopping. This is the guarantee the BEAM's signature feature lacked.

── point the checker at the annotated server ──
  machine:   account
  checked:   true   (it has an ^{:invariant})
  □(balance ≥ 0) holds: true   — proven for every balance and amount
  warnings:  ()

── remove the withdraw guard — the checker renders the failure ──
account.bl:1:1: invariant not preserved by withdraw
  1 │ (defserver ^{:invariant (>= balance 0)} account
    │ ^

── whole-program message coverage ──
  the server handles:  #{:deposit :withdraw}
  a caller sends :deposit, :withdraw, and :close …
  unhandled (latent FunctionClauseError): (:close)

  → TLA+ cannot do this: it has no callers. We check the model against
    the very code that calls it — one join over the call graph.

── the takeaway ──
  Verification is a property of the beam-lisp EVALUATOR. You wrote an
  ordinary defserver; one annotation made it verifiable; every process
  form that lowers to receive/loop/clauses inherits the same battery.

── a two-variable RELATIONAL invariant, proven jointly ──
  machine:   account
  checked:   true   (^{:invariant} couples balance and reserved)
  □(reserved ≥ 0 ∧ balance ≥ reserved) holds: true
  warnings:  ()

── the reservation-ignoring withdraw is caught ──
account.bl:1:1: invariant not preserved by withdraw
  1 │ (defserver ^{:invariant (and (>= reserved 0) (>= balance reserved))} account
    │ ^

── the single-variable seam is unchanged (N=1) ──
  □(balance ≥ 0) holds: true   checked: true

── the takeaway ──
  State is the shape the type lattice infers, not one integer. A
  relational invariant over a tuple is proven for the whole primed
  tuple at once — one define-fun, N params. Rung 1 of richer state.

── a mixed-sort (Int × Bool) invariant, proven jointly ──
  machine:   vault
  checked:   true   (fields balance:Int, frozen:Bool read from init)
  □(frozen ⇒ balance = 0) holds: true
  warnings:  ()

── a deposit that ignores the freeze is caught ──
vault.bl:1:1: invariant not preserved by deposit
  1 │ (defserver ^{:invariant (or (not frozen) (= balance 0))} vault
    │ ^

── the takeaway ──
  A field is its lattice-inferred SORT, not always Int. The bool
  `frozen` and the int `balance` are proven together — one define-fun
  over a mixed-sort state. Rung 2: type-directed SMT translation.

── capacity as an EXACT guarantee (length abstraction) ──
  machine:    worker
  checked:    true   (pending abstracted to its length)
  □(count pending ≤ 10) holds: true
  extension:  :exact   — proven for ALL traces, unbounded
  warnings:   ()

── an unguarded enqueue can overflow — caught ──
  holds: false   warning: ("invariant not preserved by enqueue")

── the honesty: the guarantee catalog ──
  exact guarantees:        (:worker-capacity :~balance-nonneg)
  approximate guarantees:  (:worker-no-duplicate :~replies-eventually)

── the takeaway ──
  A collection is its LENGTH for a capacity invariant — Rung 1 over a
  derived integer, EXACT. Content needs the elements and is a bounded
  check. The catalog says which is which; we never call bounded exact.

── z3: protocol conformance over a datatype (send only while open) ──
  machine:   conn
  checked:   true   (phase : E_phase — a z3 datatype, not an Int)
  □(sending ⇒ phase = :open) holds: true
  warnings:  ()

── datalog: the phase graph, reasoned as code-as-data ──
  edges (from→to): (["idle" "open"] ["open" "open"] ["open" "closed"])
  reachable tags : #{"closed" "idle" "open"}   (all three — no dead states)
  terminal tags  : #{"closed"}   (closed is a sink — correct)
  dead tags      : #{}   (none — every variant is reachable)

── z3: use-after-close is caught ──
conn.bl:1:1: invariant not preserved by send
  1 │ (defserver ^{:invariant (or (not sending) (= phase :open))} conn
    │ ^

── the takeaway ──
  A variant is a z3 DATATYPE: you write :open, the checker fences the
  phase to exactly {idle,open,closed} for free, and z3 proves the
  session type over it. The phase GRAPH — reachable, terminal, dead —
  is datalog over code-as-data, the same engine deadlock and message
  coverage use. z3 decides; datalog reasons about structure; neither
  does the other's job.

── point the checker at an un-annotated server ──
  machine:             account
  DISCOVERED invariant: (and (>= balance 0))
  (proven established at init AND preserved by every transition)

── the discovered invariant proves when annotated back ──
  □ (and (>= balance 0)) holds: true

── an unbounded-below machine discovers no lower bound (honest) ──
  DISCOVERED: (and (<= n 0) (<= n 1))  (no (>= n 0) — dec can go negative)

── the takeaway ──
  The invariant is abduce's search aimed at the WHOLE property, run as
  a Houdini fixpoint seeded by the state vars. No annotation needed —
  and when the shape admits none, we say so rather than invent one.
── one verify-process, every state shape ──

  scalar int  (balance ≥ 0)         ✓ holds
  tuple ints  (balance ≥ reserved)  ✓ holds
  record Int×Bool (frozen ⇒ 0)      ✓ holds
  variant/phase (send⇒open)         ✓ holds
  collection  (count ≤ 10)          ✓ holds   [exact]

── ★ invariant discovered from an UN-annotated machine ──
  discovered:  (and (>= n 0))

── the thesis ──
  state ≜ ⟦type-lattice⟧, not Int. One verify-process reads each
  field's sort from the init state and proves the invariant over the
  whole shape. int · tuple · record · variant · collection · and the
  invariant itself, discovered. The engines never changed; only the
  wire from the type phase to the SMT translator was missing.

── a Real-valued invariant, proven over real arithmetic ──
  machine:    rated
  checked:    true   (rate:Real read from the 0.05 init)
  □(rate ≥ 0.0) holds: true
  warnings:   ()

── an unguarded bump is caught in the Real theory ──
  holds:     false   (an unguarded real amount breaks it)
  warnings:  ({:line 1, :msg "invariant not preserved by bump", :col 1})

── the takeaway ──
  z3 speaks every theory; the translator now speaks Real. A field is
  its lattice-inferred SORT — :float → Real — and message amounts
  inherit the sort of the field they flow into. Same verify-process,
  new theory, zero new checker code.

── a string-content invariant, proven over the String theory ──
  machine:    auditlog
  checked:    true   (last:String read from the "evt:init" init)
  □("evt:" is a prefix of last) holds: true
  warnings:   ()

── an append that drops the prefix is caught ──
  holds:     false   (raw body need not start with evt:)
  warnings:  ({:line 1, :msg "invariant not preserved by append", :col 1})

── the takeaway ──
  Content, not count. (str-prefix? …) → (str.prefixof …), (str a b) →
  (str.++ …), (str-len s) → (str.len s). The message body is a String
  input, and the format invariant is proven on the actual text. This
  is the collection-content frontier (Gap 1), reached through a theory
  that was always in the solver — we just weren't emitting it.
── one verify-process, four z3 theories, each field its own ──

  Int    counter (n ≥ 0)        sort→theory checked= true  holds= true 
  Real   rate (rate ≥ 0.0)      sort→theory checked= true  holds= true 
  Bool   latch (done ⇒ done)    sort→theory checked= true  holds= true 
  String log (evt: prefix)      sort→theory checked= true  holds= true 

── the takeaway ──
  Four machines, four z3 theories, ONE verify-process. No per-theory
  code path — each field's tag (:int :float :bool :string) selects its
  sort (Int Real Bool String) through the functor, and message inputs
  inherit the sort of the field they flow into. The Int prison was
  ours, in the translator; z3 always spoke every theory. Removing two
  hardcoded sorts opened all of them.

── a payload variant, proven over a z3 datatype ──
  machine:   dial
  checked:   true   (mode : E_mode = paused | (on Int))
  □(on ⇒ level ≥ 0) holds: true
  warnings:  ()

── an unguarded turn-on is caught ──
  holds:     false   (a negative level can enter :on)
  warnings:  ({:line 1, :msg "invariant not preserved by turn-on", :col 1})

── the same invariant runs as ordinary bl ──
  (inv :paused)   => true   (nullary arm — vacuously true)
  (inv {:on 5})   => true   (on, level 5 — true)
  (inv {:on -1})  => false   (on, level -1 — false, the bug)

── the takeaway ──
  One structural scheme spans the whole sum-type story: a keyword is a
  nullary arm, a single-key map is a payload arm, an all-nullary field
  is the enum (demo 13). Discriminant and accessor are plain bl map ops
  that already mean the right thing, so a verified invariant is just an
  invariant you could have written anyway. z3's no-junk axiom fences
  the field to exactly its arms; the accessor is total under the guard.

── CTL AG¬fault: the provably-safe region ──
  safe states: (0 1 2 3)
  4 is unsafe — it can step to the fault; 5 is the fault itself.
  the 0→1→2→3→0 cycle never reaches 5, so it is provably safe.

── bisimulation: which states are behaviourally identical ──
  equivalence classes: ((0) (1 2 3))
  states 1,2,3 all do just :a→(a-sink); 0 alone offers :b.
  the machine minimizes from 4 states to 2 classes.

── provenance: why each element fell ──
  survivors: (1 3)
   dropped 2 — reason :even
   dropped 4 — reason :even
   dropped 5 — reason :too-big

── the takeaway ──
  One primitive — seed full, keep what survives the current survivors,
  repeat until stable — is the greatest fixpoint. It is the exact dual
  of datom's grow-from-nothing recursion, and it hosts CTL safe-regions,
  bisimulation, and (with a z3 decision) Houdini invariant discovery.
  The decision is the caller's; the shrink is the engine's.

── the dispatch topology, read as facts ──
  handled labels    : #{:close :open :send}
  always fireable    : #{:close}   (unguarded — total handlers)
  guarded-only       : #{:open :send}   (partial — a sender can be ignored)

── the guard preconditions — the spec nobody wrote down ──
   :open requires ["(= phase :idle)"]
   :send requires ["(= phase :open)"]

── a raw datalog query over the same facts ──
  labels with a phase-guard: #{:open :send}

── the takeaway ──
  Dispatch, guards, and patterns are STRUCTURE, so they are datalog —
  the same engine deadlock and message-coverage already use. The guard
  preconditions are the process's real precondition spec, extracted
  from the :when clauses rather than annotated. z3 never runs here;
  structure is not its job.

── synthesize the capacity — no annotation ──
  machine:       q5
  bounded:       true
  SYNTHESIZED capacity: 5   (guard admits at count<5, then +1 → max 5)

── an unguarded queue has no finite capacity ──
  bounded:       false
  synthesized:   nil   (nil — honestly unbounded, not a guess)

── the takeaway ──
  The typing phase built a RELATION, not a one-way checker: the same
  inductiveness query that PROVES an annotated bound also SEARCHES for
  the tightest bound when none is written. Run it forwards to check,
  backwards to synthesize. The capacity that used to be an annotation
  is now a result — and an unbounded machine is reported, never faked.

── sends that hit a GUARDED precondition (sender must establish it) ──
   client → account :withdraw requires (>= balance amt)

── sends that are DROPPED (receiver has no handler) ──
   client → account :close — no handler, silently dropped

── the composition: sends that could break the RECEIVER'S invariant ──
   client → account :withdraw
      guard:     (>= balance amt)
      protects:  (>= balance 0) — violate the guard, break this

── a raw cross-cutting datalog query ──
  processes carrying an invariant:
    account guarantees (>= balance 0)

── the takeaway ──
  Dispatch, guards, and invariants were separate within-process checks.
  Projected into ONE relation they JOIN: 'which send could break the
  receiver's invariant' is (send × receiver-clause × guard × invariant)
  — a datalog query, not a new checker. Each capability added joins with
  every prior one: N guarantees, N² questions, for the cost of projection.

── the broken account fails verification ──
  holds:    false
  warnings: ({:line 1, :msg "invariant not preserved by withdraw", :col 1})

── repair: synthesize the guard each broken transition needs ──
  nothing to repair? false
  transition :withdraw → add guard (<= amt balance)

── the repair is the most permissive sound guard ──
  chosen:       (<= amt balance)   (admits every safe withdrawal)
  all sound:    ((<= amt balance) (<= amt 0) (>= balance amt) (>= 0 amt))
  rejected the degenerate (<= amt 0) — sound but forbids the useful case

── round trip: the repaired machine verifies ──
  with the synthesized guard, holds: true

── the takeaway ──
  A checker that is a RELATION runs both ways. Forward it proves a
  guard sufficient; backward it SYNTHESIZES the guard, and the weakest
  sound one is the repair. The forward checker confirms it — synthesize
  backward, verify forward. (The repair is as expressive as its
  candidate vocabulary: relational (op a b) guards here; a richer
  vocabulary would synthesize compound guards like (<= (+ r amt) bal).)

── a freshness-guarded set stays duplicate-free ──
  machine:   uniqset
  checked:   true   (items : Array Int Int)
  distinct (no duplicates ever) holds: true
  guarantee: :approximate   (quantified array reasoning — sound but not always decidable)

── an unguarded add can insert a duplicate — caught ──
  distinct holds: false
  warnings:       ({:line 1, :msg "invariant not preserved by add", :col 1})

── the takeaway ──
  A collection is a z3 Array, not a length. 'No duplicates' is a
  content invariant — ∀ i<j. a[i] ≠ a[j] — proven over the elements.
  The freshness guard is what makes it inductive; without it, z3 finds
  the value that collides. Length says how big; the Array says what's
  inside. Gap 1, closed by the theory that was always in the solver.

── a job that always eventually completes (AF done) ──
  machine:       job
  from phase:    pending
  ◇done (every path reaches done) holds: true
  states that reach done: ("done" "pending" "running")

── a job that can retry forever never guarantees completion ──
  ◇done holds: false   (a running→running loop starves)
  states that reach done: []   (running is NOT among them)

── the takeaway ──
  Safety (□) shrinks from everything, keeping states that never touch
  bad — a greatest fixpoint. Liveness (◇) grows from the goal, adding
  states whose every future reaches it — a LEAST fixpoint. Same phase
  graph, dual directions. With both, the tier proves the two halves of
  temporal logic natively — no JVM, no TLA⁺ export. A starvation loop
  is caught precisely because it is NOT in the least fixpoint.

── least fixpoint: what is reachable from 4 (grow from nothing) ──
  reachable from 4: (0 1 2 3 4 5)   (4 can reach the fault 5)

── greatest fixpoint: the provably-safe region (shrink from everything) ──
  safe states: (0 1 2 3)
  computed in 2 rounds, 7 checks (semi-naive delta)
  4 is unsafe (it can reach the fault); the 0→1→2→3 cycle is safe.

── the takeaway ──
  One engine, two directions. Least fixpoint grows from a seed and
  answers reachability, closure, connected components. Greatest fixpoint
  shrinks from the full set and answers safe regions, bisimulation, and
  — with a z3 decision per step — Houdini invariant discovery. Same
  semi-naive delta accelerates both; both return a relation. The
  fixpoint the verifier needs no longer lives outside the database.

── verify by quoted name: 'examples.guards/account ──
  (sys/verify 'examples.guards/account) → :ok

── the clause's binder is its own name for the state ──
  checked: true   □(b ≥ 0) holds: false  — binder `b` substituted for `balance` and proven

── a buggy server with the clause is CAUGHT ──
wallet.bl:1:1: invariant not preserved by spend
  1 │ (defserver wallet
    │ ^

── the meta spelling still verifies (same engine, both spellings) ──
  ^{:invariant} meta — checked: true   holds: true

── honest negatives ──
  unknown name → :unchecked  (source not found: :unchecked, never a silent pass)
  no invariant at all → checked: false  (the checker stays silent on what it cannot model)

  → one clause in the server, one call at the gate: □Inv proven
    from source, before the process ever boots.
