── exists = the mock (∃ a value the spec admits) ──
  ∃ temp>50 in [-90,60]:  {:engine :z3, :witness 51, :modality :proven}
  ∃ rate in (0.3,0.4):    {:engine :z3, :witness 0.35, :modality :proven}
  ∃ sk_-prefixed token:   {:engine :z3, :witness "sk_ABCED", :modality :proven}

── for-all = the property (∀ input, the property holds) ──
  ∀ n≥0 in [0,100] (z3):      {:engine :z3, :modality :proven}
  ∀ n<50 in [0,100] refuted:  {:engine :z3, :witness 50, :modality :refuted}

── covers = the lint (∀ input, some clause handles it) ──
  classify over [-5,5] (gap):      {:guards [(pos? n) (neg? n)], :engine :enumerate, :witness 0, :modality :refuted}
  fixed over [-5,5] (PROVEN):      {:guards [(pos? n) (neg? n) (zero? n)], :engine :enumerate, :modality :proven, :tried 11}

── the modality never lets a sample pose as a proof ──
  ∀ int?(x) over gen/any:  {:engine :sample, :witness 0, :modality :refuted, :shrunk 0}
  → :witnessed/:refuted by SAMPLE — gen/any spans every type, so the
    float 1.5 witnesses the gap automatically; no proof is claimed.

── the point ──
  One verb, one generator algebra, three engines. exists is the mock,
  for-all is the property test, covers is the lint — and each verdict
  says whether z3 PROVED it, exhaustion PROVED it, or a sample merely
  WITNESSED it. The confidence is in the value, not a footnote.

── int — the Int theory ──
  ∃ n in [1,10], n>7:  {:engine :z3, :witness 8, :modality :proven}

── real — the Real theory, exact rationals ──
  ∃ r in (0,1), r>0.9:  {:engine :z3, :witness 1.0, :modality :proven}

── string — the String theory, structured values ──
  ∃ s prefixed sk_, len≥8:  {:engine :z3, :witness "sk_ABCED", :modality :proven}

── bool — finite, so ∀ is EXHAUSTIVELY proven ──
  ∀ b, b or not b:  {:engine :z3, :modality :proven}

── one-of — a finite value set; ∀ enumerates, ∃ picks ──
  ∀ code<600:  {:engine :z3, :modality :proven}
  ∀ code=200 (refuted):  {:engine :z3, :witness 500, :modality :refuted}

── the SAME predicate (n≥0), three generators, three modalities ──
  int [0,3]  → z3 proves:       {:engine :z3, :modality :proven}
  one-of {0,1,2} → enumerate:   {:engine :z3, :modality :proven}
  any → sample (witnessed):     {:engine :sample, :witness -1, :modality :refuted, :shrunk -1}

── the point ──
  You did not choose an engine — you chose a GENERATOR, and the
  strongest engine it supports followed. int and one-of prove or
  enumerate; gen/any can only sample, and says so. The generator is
  the seam between the three engines the whole system runs on.

── an error for exactly one law — requested by the law it breaks ──
  wrong for law 0 (temp≥-40):  {:engine :z3, :witness -50, :modality :proven}
  wrong for law 1 (temp≤60):   {:engine :z3, :witness 61, :modality :proven}
  wrong for law 2 (temp≥-50):  {:none true, :engine :z3, :witness nil, :modality :proven}

── the whole fault space at once ──
  law 0 → modality proven  isolated fault: -50
  law 1 → modality proven  isolated fault: 61
  law 2 → modality proven  IMPLIED (redundant — proven un-breakable alone)

── the point ──
  The fault space is not a second tool — it is exists with a negated
  law. z3 either hands you an isolated fault (wrong for exactly that
  law) or PROVES the law cannot be broken alone, which means the
  contract stated it twice. The redundancy is proven, not guessed —
  the modality carries the finding.
── a contract IS a mock ──
   one valid response:   {:code 200, :temp 0}

── the mock is a deterministic SERVER ──
   request 200 →   {:code 200, :temp -36}
   request 200 again →   {:code 200, :temp -36}
   request 250 →   {:code 250, :temp -36}
   same key ⇒ same response:  true

── the boundaries, generated for free ──
   coldest valid temp:   {:code 200, :temp -40}
   hottest valid temp:   {:code 200, :temp 60}

── adversarial: a response the contract FORBIDS ──
   an invalid response:   {:code 0, :temp 61}
   still inside the contract?  false

── the point ──
  A mock is not written — it is DERIVED from the same contract the real
  impl is checked against, so the two answer to one predicate and are
  interchangeable. Determinism makes it a server; the optimizer makes the
  edges free; the negation makes the adversarial case provably wrong.
── z3 generates the isolated faults ──
   break law 0 alone:   {:v -50}
   break law 1 alone:   {:v 61}
   break law 2 alone:   :IMPLIED-cannot-break-alone

── datalog analyses independence ──
   laws:   {:implied [2], :independent [0 1], :undecidable []}
   law 2 is z3-PROVEN redundant — a finding about the SPEC, not a guess.

── coverage is a not-join query ──
   covered clauses:   [0]
   uncovered (blind spots):   [1 2]

── a server that injects errors by NAME ──
   normal request 42:   {:reason nil, :value {:v 42}, :ok? true}
   injected error :boom:   {:reason 0, :value {:v 101}, :ok? false}

── the point ──
  The fault space is not a second tool — it is ∃ with a negated law. z3
  hands you a value wrong for exactly one nameable law (or PROVES the law
  can't be broken alone ⇒ redundant); datalog then answers coverage and
  redundancy as ordinary queries over the fault set.
── a property that HOLDS ──
   ∀ n∈[0,100]. 2n ≥ n :   {:holds true, :tried 101}

── a property that is REFUTED, then SHRUNK ──
   ∀ n. 2n > n  (false at 0):   {:crash false, :result 0, :holds false, :witness 0, :shrunk 0}
   the witness shrank to 0 — the SIMPLEST failing input, not the first.

── a crash is a first-class outcome ──
   divide by zero:   {:crash true, :result nil, :holds false, :witness 1, :shrunk 0}

── the generator picks the domain — any-of spans types ──
   ∀ n. int?(n) over any-of:   {:crash false, :result 1.5, :holds false, :witness 1.5, :shrunk 1.5}
   any-of yields a float/string/… so the type property is refuted — no
   hand-built mixed domain to remember.

── a multi-arg property draws from tuple-of ──
   ∀ a,b∈[0,20]. a-b ≥ 0 :   {:crash false, :result -1, :holds false, :witness [0 1], :shrunk [0 1]}

── the point ──
  A property is the code's own behaviour, checked over a generated domain
  and shrunk to its minimal witness. Same generator algebra as the core's
  exists/for-all — one seam feeds the symbolic, exhaustive, and behavioural
  checks alike.

── a nullary enum: proven symbolically, refuted with a projected witness ──
  for-all t. t∈{pending,running,done}:  {:engine :z3, :modality :proven}
  for-all t. t≠:running:  {:engine :z3, :witness :running, :modality :refuted}

── the same law outside the fragment — exhaustion proves it instead ──
  for-all t. (keyword? t):  {:engine :enumerate, :modality :proven, :tried 2}

── a payload enum: z3 answers exists by building the {:on n} record itself ──
  exists m. (:on m) present:  {:engine :z3, :witness {:on 2}, :modality :proven}
  for-all m. the arm law:  {:engine :z3, :modality :proven}

── length laws: proven over every vector, refuted with the offending length ──
  for-all c∈len[0,5]. (count c) ≤ 5:  {:engine :z3, :modality :proven}
  for-all c∈len[0,9]. (count c) ≤ 5:  {:engine :z3, :witness [0 1 2 3 4 5], :modality :refuted}
  exists c∈len[0,3]. (count c) > 3:  {:none true, :engine :z3, :witness nil, :modality :proven}
  for-all c∈len[0,9]. 7 ∉ c:  (falls to sample — content is open)  {:engine :sample, :witness [0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8], :modality :refuted, :shrunk [0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8]}

── a joint query over Int × enum: one z3 call, both datatypes ──
  for-all p. (nth p 1) ≠ :a:  {:engine :z3, :witness nil, :modality :refuted}
  …but coll × enum needs variants AND lengths in one pass — none
  exists, so that tuple samples, and the verdict says :witnessed.

── the point ──
  A theory is not a feature of the solver — it is a GENERATOR. Bind the
  domain on the bl rung, declare it to z3 on the symbolic rung, project
  witnesses back to bl values, and every quantifier you already have
  (exists / for-all / covers / faults) speaks the new sort for free.
